So for Trees you want Comparable (and thus Comparators) for Hash you want hashCode and equal. This we already know from Java

Now let's try to make sense out of what C++ wants:

For your own structs:
 -- TreeSets (aka std::set) is easy, just implement the "bool operator<(const T&) const" and you are all set
 -- HashSets (aka std::unordered_set), you need to implement "bool operator=(const T& rhs) const"
    But this just takes care of the equality. You also need the hash function. For this you can either do
    struct A {
        struct HashCode {
     	   size_t operator()(const T&rhs) const { ... }
	}
    }

===== OR =====

    namespace std {
       template <> struct hash<T> {
       	  size_t operator()(const T&rhs) const { ... }
       }
    }

   std::unordered_set<T, T::HashCode> for the first approach and std::unordered_set<T> for the second one

